{"sourceSha256": "1681c1c83751ad42e062b6d11da8d7f88852ac19a8abc844d6060b295a617ed5", "text": "Information\nP Flag question\n\nParagraph 3\n\nUntil now, RLS and its potential relationship with suicide had not been studied in any depth. The Yale\nteam investigated the frequency of lifetime suicidal behaviour in 198 patients with severe RLS and 164\ncontrols. All participants completed the Suicidal Behaviour Questionnaire-revised (SBQ-R) and the Brief\nLifetime Depression Scale. RLS and controls were similar in age (mean age, 51), income, and gender.\nCompared with controls, patients with RLS were more often white (96% vs 88%), less often had higher\neducation (84% vs 96%), were more often married (72% vs 60%), and were less often employed or\nretired (80% vs 90%). Significantly more patients with RLS than controls were at high suicide risk\n(SBQ-R score = 7) and had lifetime suicidal thoughts or behaviour, independent of depression history.\n\nQuestion 3 What did the Yale team learn from their investigations?\n\nNot yet answered\n\nMarked out of 1.00 lect one:\n\nA. Some people in the control group had previously suffered from RLS.\nP Flag question\nB. The likelihood of someone developing RLS depends on various factors.\n\nC. Answers to the questionnaires didn’t provide a lot of useful data about RLS.\n\nD. A person with RLS is more likely to attempt suicide than someone without it.", "pages": [{"number": 1, "text": "Information\nP Flag question\n\nParagraph 3\n\nUntil now, RLS and its potential relationship with suicide had not been studied in any depth. The Yale\nteam investigated the frequency of lifetime suicidal behaviour in 198 patients with severe RLS and 164\ncontrols. All participants completed the Suicidal Behaviour Questionnaire-revised (SBQ-R) and the Brief\nLifetime Depression Scale. RLS and controls were similar in age (mean age, 51), income, and gender.\nCompared with controls, patients with RLS were more often white (96% vs 88%), less often had higher\neducation (84% vs 96%), were more often married (72% vs 60%), and were less often employed or\nretired (80% vs 90%). Significantly more patients with RLS than controls were at high suicide risk\n(SBQ-R score = 7) and had lifetime suicidal thoughts or behaviour, independent of depression history.\n\nQuestion 3 What did the Yale team learn from their investigations?\n\nNot yet answered\n\nMarked out of 1.00 lect one:\n\nA. Some people in the control group had previously suffered from RLS.\nP Flag question\nB. The likelihood of someone developing RLS depends on various factors.\n\nC. Answers to the questionnaires didn’t provide a lot of useful data about RLS.\n\nD. A person with RLS is more likely to attempt suicide than someone without it.", "extraction": "ocr", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 2800.0, "height": 1645.0, "blocks": [{"text": "Information\nP Flag question", "lines": [{"text": "Information", "x": 0.015, "y": 0.0, "width": 0.06893, "height": 0.01702, "confidence": 0.304, "words": [{"text": "Information", "x": 0.015, "width": 0.06893, "confidence": 0.304}], "uncertainWords": ["Information"]}, {"text": "P Flag question", "x": 0.01643, "y": 0.04316, "width": 0.095, "height": 0.02553, "confidence": 0.645, "words": [{"text": "P", "x": 0.01643, "width": 0.01214, "confidence": 0.0}, {"text": "Flag", "x": 0.03643, "width": 0.02321, "confidence": 0.967}, {"text": "question", "x": 0.06286, "width": 0.04857, "confidence": 0.969}], "uncertainWords": ["P"]}]}, {"text": "Paragraph 3", "lines": [{"text": "Paragraph 3", "x": 0.17107, "y": 0.0997, "width": 0.08929, "height": 0.02979, "confidence": 0.968, "words": [{"text": "Paragraph", "x": 0.17107, "width": 0.07607, "confidence": 0.966}, {"text": "3", "x": 0.25214, "width": 0.00821, "confidence": 0.97}], "uncertainWords": []}]}, {"text": "Until now, RLS and its potential relationship with suicide had not been studied in any depth. The Yale\nteam investigated the frequency of lifetime suicidal behaviour in 198 patients w